  q(ELEC,'U.S. wall power is 120 V RMS. What is its approximate peak voltage?','170 V',['85 V','120 V','240 V'],'C','Peak = RMS × 1.414 (√2), so 120 × 1.414 ≈ 170 V. Meters normally display RMS values.',MET),
  q(REAS,'A defibrillator shows “pads not connected” though the pads are on the patient. What should be checked first?','The pad cable connection',['The printer paper','The screen brightness','The date and time'],'D','A loose or damaged cable or connector is the most common cause of this message.',AAMI),
  q(REAS,'A monitor shows a heart rate twice the patient’s actual pulse. What is a likely cause?','Tall T waves counted as beats',['A failing battery pack','A low room temperature','A loose SpO₂ finger probe'],'A','Selecting a different lead or adjusting the gain can stop the monitor from double-counting.',AAMI),
  q(REAS,'An infusion pump alarms “upstream occlusion.” What is the most likely cause?','A closed clamp above the pump',['A kinked line at the patient','Air in the drip chamber','A full IV bag'],'B','Upstream means between the bag and the pump; check clamps, spikes and kinks there.',AAMI),
  q(REAS,'A thermal printer on an ECG cart feeds paper but prints nothing. What is a likely cause?','Paper loaded upside down',['A failed ECG amplifier','Low patient heart rate','A loose lead wire'],'C','Thermal paper has only one heat-sensitive side; reload it correctly before replacing parts.',AAMI),
  q(REAS,'A patient scale reads 2 kg high for every patient. What should be done?','Zero and calibrate it',['Subtract 2 kg by memory','Replace the patients’ gowns','Ignore the small error'],'D','A consistent offset suggests a zero or calibration error; check it against certified test weights.',AAMI),
  q(REAS,'An ultrasound image shows a dark vertical stripe in the same place on every scan. What is likely?','A damaged transducer element',['A wrong patient name entry','Too much coupling gel','Low room light levels'],'A','Dead elements in the probe cause dropout lines; probes can be tested and replaced.',AAMI),
  q(REAS,'Several monitors drop off the central station at the same moment. What should be checked first?','The shared network switch',['Each monitor’s battery','Each patient’s leads','Each monitor’s volume'],'B','A failure common to many devices at once points to shared infrastructure, such as a switch or power.',AAMI),
  q(REAS,'A ventilator alarms for low exhaled volume, and air is heard at the patient’s mouth. What is likely?','A leak around the tube cuff',['A blocked expiratory filter','A high oxygen setting','A kinked oxygen hose'],'C','A leaking endotracheal tube cuff lets delivered air escape, so less volume returns to the ventilator.',AAMI),
  q(REAS,'A heated humidifier stays cold even though its heater light is on. What is the likely fault?','A failed heater or sensor',['A full water chamber','A wrong patient setting','A loose oxygen mask'],'D','The control signal is present, so the fault is likely in the heater element or temperature sensing.',AAMI),
  q(REAS,'A technician finds a device in use with an overdue preventive maintenance sticker. What should happen?','Complete the PM before reuse',['Remove the sticker','Let it run until it fails','Change the due date'],'A','Overdue devices should be inspected and tested according to the equipment management plan.',AAMI),
  q(REAS,'A nurse reports an intermittent fault that the technician cannot reproduce. What is the best next step?','Gather details and monitor use',['Close the work order now','Tell the nurse it is fine','Replace the whole device'],'B','Asking when and how the fault occurs, checking logs and watching use help find intermittent problems.',AAMI),
  q(REAS,'A portable suction unit stops pulling after its canister fills. What has most likely happened?','The overflow valve closed',['The motor burned out','The power cord failed','The vacuum gauge broke'],'C','The float or shutoff valve protects the pump from fluid; empty or replace the canister.',MET),
  q(IT,'An address such as 192.168.1.20 is which type of IP address?','Private',['Public','Broadcast','Loopback'],'D','Private ranges are used inside a facility and are not routed directly on the internet.',AAMI),
  q(IT,'HTTPS web traffic normally uses which port?','443',['21','25','8080'],'A','Port 80 is unencrypted HTTP; port 443 is HTTPS, which encrypts data in transit.',AAMI),
  q(IT,'What does encryption do to data?','Scrambles it for key holders only',['Deletes it after a set time','Copies it to a backup','Compresses it to save space'],'B','Encryption protects patient data at rest and in transit if a device or network is compromised.',AAMI),
  q(IT,'What is a “zero-day” vulnerability?','A flaw with no patch yet',['A flaw fixed the same day','A virus that deletes dates','A backup made at midnight'],'C','Attackers can exploit zero-days before a fix exists, so network segmentation and monitoring are vital.',AAMI),
  q(IT,'For medical device cybersecurity, what does SBOM stand for?','Software Bill of Materials',['System Backup of Memory','Secure Boot Operating Mode','Standard Board of Monitors'],'D','An SBOM lists software components so hospitals can tell whether a device is affected by a known flaw.',FDA),
  q(IT,'What does the 3-2-1 backup rule recommend?','3 copies, 2 media, 1 offsite',['3 backups per day for 2 weeks','3 users, 2 passwords, 1 server','3 servers in 2 rooms, 1 on'],'A','Keeping copies on different media and off-site protects data from ransomware and disasters.',AAMI),
  q(IT,'What does an interface engine do in a hospital network?','Converts messages between systems',['Charges every device battery','Prints patient ID wristbands','Cools the main server room'],'B','Interface engines route and convert HL7 and other messages so devices and the EHR can share data.',AAMI)
